uEcho 1.4.4
==========

Changes since the published 1.4.3 release
---------------------------------------

This is a maintenance release with message parsing robustness fixes and build
fixes for newer toolchains. There are no API changes.

- uecho_message_setopcep() no longer writes through a NULL pointer when the
  property array allocation fails (#22).
- uecho_message_parse() reports allocation failures of the OPC/OPCSet/OPCGet
  property arrays instead of continuing with an empty property list (#23).
- Test builds no longer require the Boost.System library, which Boost 1.89+
  no longer provides. src/uecho/net/interface_function.c includes <unistd.h>
  on all non-Windows builds so CMake builds compile with GCC 14+, which rejects
  implicit function declarations. Ubuntu 26.04 CI runners (Boost 1.90) build
  and pass the full test suite again (#24).

The shipped configure script is regenerated so that it reports 1.4.4 and
includes the #24 Autotools change.

uEcho 1.4.3
==========

Changes since the published 1.4.2 release
---------------------------------------

The C core now provides allocation-free scalar EDT conversion using optional
constraints generated from the existing MRA 1.3.0 data. The generated codec
metadata is independent of the optional original JSON metadata; ordinary C
builds keep both disabled unless requested. CMake, Autotools compilation flags
and ESP-IDF Kconfig support codec-enabled and metadata-disabled builds.

The supported subset is signed/unsigned 8/16/32-bit numbers, exact 1/2/4-byte
state enums, pure local refs, and one numeric branch combined with exact,
same-width, disjoint state/sentinel branches. Results retain raw EDT, unit,
read-only annotations and distinct unsupported, invalid, unknown, number, state
and special outcomes. AC class 0130 B0 includes other (0x40) and 0x41-0x45;
B3 supports 0-50 Celsius plus undefined FD, and BB supports signed temperature
plus unmeasurable 7E. CGEchoValue is a thin Objective-C adapter to the C codec.
See doc/edt-codec.md for the C and HomeKit migration examples.

Since 1.4.2, the Objective-C wrapper has also been rebuilt around asynchronous
CGEchoController, immutable session snapshots, tri-state maps, cancellation,
strict response matching and no automatic retries. Standard metadata, optional
original MRA schemas, the in-tree SwiftUI example, POSIX shutdown fixes and
stricter format-2 map count validation are included. See ChangeLog for PRs.

Compatibility and limits
------------------------

The old borrowed-pointer Objective-C wrapper API was removed. Existing ObjC
applications must migrate to the new API (doc/objc.md); macOS 12+ and iOS 15.4+
are required by the new framework. Compile all C sources with consistent
metadata flags because they change internal table layouts. Standard mandatory
flags never establish real device capability or permission.

Complex constraints are explicitly unsupported: ref siblings/overrides,
overlapping or duplicate unions, multiple numeric branches, state ranges,
arrays/objects/bitmap/level, numeric enums, unknown types, multipleOf,
coefficient-dependent scaling and implicit overflow/underflow codes. Unsupported
conversion preserves raw bytes instead of guessing. Missing/external/cyclic
refs and malformed JSON-pointer escapes reject generation.

No new app AC-write path is enabled. Session-bound property descriptors over
the union of Get/Set/INF maps, typed write/readback, 1/17-byte installation
location decoding and conditional access metadata are follow-up work.
Validation uses unit tests, fake transport, sanitizers and build matrices;
physical-device codec interoperability has not been verified. Real-device smoke
tests were not run as part of this release preparation.

Publication
-----------

This PR prepares 1.4.3; it does not create a tag or publish a Release. The
published 1.4.2 tag and Release remain unchanged. After PR #21 is confirmed
merged, verify the merged commit and its CI/Sonar results, recheck that 1.4.3
is still unused, then create an annotated 1.4.3 tag on that exact commit and
publish matching GitHub Release notes. Do not tag the pre-merge PR branch.
